Q: How to return the result of CodeIgniter&#39;s update() from a model method A: <p>In model <code>update_site_settings</code> function check the query is executed or not, If executed successfully <code>return true</code> else <code>return false</code></p>

<pre><code>public function update_site_settings($data,$where_cond,$value,$table_name)
{
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;set($data);
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;where($where_cond,$value);
    if($this-&gt;db-&gt;update($table_name,$data))
    {
        return true;
    }
    else
    {
        return false;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: MDPDF: Markdown to PDF conversion with dynamic footer or header A: <p>You’re likely running into a limitation of how mdpdf handles footer HTML.</p>
<p>The HTML passed via --footer is usually not rendered like a normal web page with a full DOM lifecycle, so this script:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
    var dt = new Date();
    document.getElementById(&quot;datetime&quot;).innerHTML = dt.toLocaleDateString();&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>may execute before the &lt;span id=&quot;datetime&quot;&gt; element exists. In that case, the date never gets inserted into the footer, while the underlying PDF<br />
renderer may still inject its own default date somewhere else, which is why you see:</p>
<ul>
<li>the footer text at the bottom</li>
<li>the date in the upper-left corner</li>
<li>and not in your localized format</li>
</ul>
<p>The safest approach is to avoid JavaScript in the footer entirely and write the date into the footer HTML before calling mdpdf.</p>
<p>For example, first verify that a static footer works:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;center style=&quot;font-size:10px&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;FOOTER with locale date: 21/03/2026&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you really want to try JavaScript, you can move the script after the HTML and wait for the DOM to load:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;center style=&quot;font-size:10px&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;FOOTER with locale date: &lt;span id=&quot;datetime&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
    window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
        document.getElementById('datetime').textContent =
          new Date().toLocaleDateString();
      });
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>However, whether this works depends on the PDF engine used by mdpdf, and header/footer templates in these tools often have very limited JavaScript<br />
support.</p>
<p>So in practice, if you need a dynamic localized date, the better solution is usually to generate the footer HTML file with the date already filled in<br />
before running mdpdf.</p>
<p>Also, if you want a quick way to test Markdown-to-PDF conversion in the browser, you can try <a href="https://mdpdf.net" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mdpdf</a> — it provides an online Markdown to<br />
PDF conversion workflow and may be useful for experimenting with layout and output.</p>
